{
  "object": "zillow_surname_keywords",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "purpose": "Keywords for the ?name= parameter of zillow.agents.list. ?name= matches the SURNAME (measured: every agent returned for ?name=kim carries Kim as a last name). The directory serves at most 25 pages (~375 agents) per query, so a common surname in a large metro IS truncated and that is accepted — the goal is maximum reach, not completeness, because no better partition exists. More keywords means more agents, roughly linearly.",
  "how_to_use": [
    "Run each keyword as its own zillow.agents.list job: /professionals/real-estate-agent-reviews/<region>/?name=<surname>&page=N.",
    "Page 1 returns results_found AND 15 agents, so evaluating a keyword costs nothing extra — the probe is the harvest.",
    "Walk pages while has_more, and stop at page 25 whatever the pager says; past 25 Zillow serves page 25 again.",
    "Read `truncated` / `unreachable_estimate` on the envelope to know what a keyword could not reach. Splitting the REGION is the only way to recover those agents; splitting is optional, not required.",
    "Dedupe across keywords on encoded_zuid. Surnames are disjoint in principle, but region overlap and team pages are not.",
    "Drop a keyword for a region once results_found < 15 — one request for less than a full page."
  ],
  "regional_note": "Surname frequency differs enormously between metros: Kim and Cho are large in Los Angeles and absent in Boise; Nguyen and Tran dominate Orange County and Houston; Garcia and Hernandez lead Miami and San Antonio; Cohen and Levy matter in New York. Reorder the groups per region rather than running all of them everywhere. A no-keyword query on the region returns results_found for the whole metro — the denominator for measuring coverage — and its first pages sample the surnames actually present in that market.",
